<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lough Corrib</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lough Corrib is the largest lake in the Republic of Ireland, lying north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> city in Connacht. It divided the lowlands of east Galway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connemara">Connemara</a> to the west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oflaherty">O'Flahertys</a> were originally lords of Muintir Murchada east of the lake, but in the 13th century the Anglo-Norman de Burghs drove them west, where they ruled Iar Connacht beyond its shores.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lough-corrib.jpg" alt="Lough Corrib" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JoachimKohler-HB · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_on_Lough_Corrib,_south_of_Clonbur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
